# Keep the serverless bundle small — runtime only needs:
# api/, gencheck/, contracts/, portal/, final_contract.json,
# benchmark_fees.json, requirements.txt, vercel.json
.venv*
examples
artifacts
benchmarks
demo
scripts
tests
*.md
# ...but the agent skill IS part of the served site — it is the /skill.md
# endpoint. Without this negation `*.md` strips it from the bundle and the
# headline "point your agent here" URL 404s in production.
!portal/static/skill.md
# pyproject.toml exists for `pip install`, NOT for the deploy. Vercel resolves
# Python dependencies from pyproject.toml or requirements.txt, and it takes
# pyproject.toml when it is present — so leaving it in the bundle made the
# build install only the LIBRARY deps from [project.dependencies]
# (genlayer-py, mcp) and skip fastapi/uvicorn, which live in the `portal`
# extra. portal/app.py imports fastapi at module level, so the function died
# on cold start with FUNCTION_INVOCATION_FAILED and took the static frontend
# down with it (the ASGI app never booted to mount it).
# Excluding it keeps the deploy on requirements.txt, which lists everything
# the portal needs. If you add a runtime dependency, add it to BOTH files.
pyproject.toml
# Claude Code plugin + marketplace manifests. These exist for `pip install` and
# `/plugin install`, not for the deploy — the serverless runtime never reads
# them, and `.vercelignore`'s whole job is keeping the bundle to what the
# runtime actually needs. Same reasoning as pyproject.toml above.
.claude-plugin
plugins
.git
node_modules
__pycache__
